WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.362
Stage 1, the test stage, has nine steps.

2
00:00:03.362 --> 00:00:06.424
Sign in each reader,
then run the pre-flight.

3
00:00:06.424 --> 00:00:10.253
Then the staged enable: read each
enabled provider on its own,

4
00:00:10.253 --> 00:00:14.234
cross-check it against the provider's
own command, and record it,

5
00:00:14.234 --> 00:00:19.363
before the providers run together, because
a failure in one would hide the others.

6
00:00:19.363 --> 00:00:24.180
Route the full collection as a dry run, and
have the service desk read the payloads:

7
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.559
every security advisory and
retirement should have one,

8
00:00:27.559 --> 00:00:30.938
with an assignment group the
change manager recognizes.

9
00:00:30.938 --> 00:00:35.526
Run the smoke test, four checks with
their expected results written down first.

10
00:00:35.526 --> 00:00:37.176
Then the one write to a cloud:

11
00:00:37.176 --> 00:00:42.272
the Service Health alert policy from
Microsoft Learn's guidance on deploying alerts

12
00:00:42.272 --> 00:00:45.359
at scale with Azure Policy,
at the test subscription,

13
00:00:45.359 --> 00:00:49.450
applied only with the hash a reviewer
who isn't the operator approved.

14
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:53.609
Schedule the weekly run with Windows
Task Scheduler's own cmdlets,

15
00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:55.500
and check the stage's evidence.

16
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.675
Finally, rehearse the rollback.

17
00:00:57.675 --> 00:01:01.431
Deleting the policy assignment
doesn't remove the resources

18
00:01:01.431 --> 00:01:05.180
the remediation created; the
change record decides on those.
